{
 "thread": "98Nqk5v8Rhc",
 "subject": "Missing = \"ML\" vs missing = \"FIML\"",
 "messages_listed": 6,
 "last_activity": "2024-07-10T04:49:13Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "jk1DW5PSAQAJ",
   "from": "Maximiliano Escaffi",
   "author": "6bc409e1d97c5d21",
   "date": "2024-07-04T15:06:13Z",
   "subject": "Missing = \"ML\" vs missing = \"FIML\"",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "Dear all, <div><br></div><div>I hope you are well.</div><div><br></div><div>I just have a simple questions about how to request Full Information ML for missing data in Lavaan. The tutorial says that to use Full Information ML, missing = &quot;ML&quot; is required in the function. Particularly, it says that:</div><div><br></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(55,58,60);font-family:&quot;Source Sans Pro&quot;,-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,Roboto,&quot;Helvetica Neue&quot;,Arial,sans-serif,&quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;;font-size:17px\">If the data contain missing values, the default behavior is listwise deletion. If the missing mechanism is MCAR (missing completely at random) or MAR (missing at random), the lavaan package provides case-wise (or ‘full information’) maximum likelihood estimation. You can turn this feature on, by using the argument </span><span style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;box-sizing:border-box;font-size:0.875em;direction:ltr;unicode-bidi:bidi-override;color:rgb(151,83,184);background-color:rgb(247,247,247);padding:0.2em\">missing = &quot;ML&quot;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(55,58,60);font-family:&quot;Source Sans Pro&quot;,-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,Roboto,&quot;Helvetica Neue&quot;,Arial,sans-serif,&quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;;font-size:17px\"> when calling the fitting function. An unrestricted (h1) model will automatically be estimated, so that all common fit indices are available.</span><br></div><div><br></div><div>However, I&#39;ve seen people here (and elsewhere) suggesting that FIML is actually requested when doing missing = &quot;FIML&quot;. For example, in this question in Research Gate, Holger says that to request FIML estimator, one needs to use missing = &quot;FIML&quot; (<a href=\"https://www.researchgate.net/post/How-to-resolve-an-issue-in-R-with-lavaan-installed-using-the-FIML-function\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://www.researchgate.net/post/How-to-resolve-an-issue-in-R-with-lavaan-installed-using-the-FIML-function&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792414418133000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1gliuCrMuBuBlTSdWApkYD\">https://www.researchgate.net/post/How-to-resolve-an-issue-in-R-with-lavaan-installed-using-the-FIML-function</a>).</div><div><br></div><div>Can someone help clarify this issue? Specifically, is FIML requested with missing = &quot;FIML&quot; or &quot;ML&quot; or both? And if there is any difference, what is the difference?</div><div><br></div><div>Kind regards,</div><div>Maximiliano</div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "l54eyo9IAAAJ",
   "from": "Terrence Jorgensen",
   "author": "3fe90052fbf2f899",
   "date": "2024-07-05T22:15:29Z",
   "subject": "Re: Missing = \"ML\" vs missing = \"FIML\"",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div><blockquote style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div>is FIML requested with missing = &quot;FIML&quot; or &quot;ML&quot; or both? </div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div><div>Either.  They are &quot;alias&quot; references to the same method: calculating the (log-)likelihood for the set of variables observed per row of data.</div><div><br></div><div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(33,33,33);font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-size:10pt;line-height:1.2\">Terrence D. Jorgensen </span><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"color:rgb(33,33,33);font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-size:10pt;line-height:1.2\">   (he, him, his)</span><span style=\"color:rgb(33,33,33);font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-size:10pt;line-height:1.2\"><br>Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics<br>Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam<br><a href=\"https://eur04.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Furldefense.com%2Fv3%2F__http%3A%2Fwww.uva.nl%2Fprofile%2Ft.d.jorgensen__%3B!!D9dNQwwGXtA!RdmdUd4WPRh7g7WAPQViCxUMWWjfCFpYPBs_GolbBQ-8m6MZFr9q93hgVEl4SnZ0IqPtX17qKMdVIrknwgORIVJK-9w%24&amp;data=05%...%40uva.nl%7C787cddbcbb304d36cd2808dc896574a6%7Ca0f1cacd618c4403b94576fb3d6874e5%7C0%7C0%7C638536316050859803%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJWIjoiMC4wLjAwMDAiLCJQIjoiV2luMzIiLCJBTiI6Ik1haWwiLCJXVCI6Mn0%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp;sdata=m0lXbiRM61LU8SlXmHCEAbZGCI%2F39ZSVK%2FOV6SuCMpY%3D&amp;reserved=0\" title=\"Original URL:\nhttps://urldefense.com/v3/__http:/www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen__;!!D9dNQwwGXtA!RdmdUd4WPRh7g7WAPQViCxUMWWjfCFpYPBs_GolbBQ-8m6MZFr9q93hgVEl4SnZ0IqPtX17qKMdVIrknwgORIVJK-9w$\n\nClick to follow link.\" rel=\"nofollow\" style=\"text-decoration-line:underline;color:rgb(0,120,215)\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://eur04.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url%3Dhttps%253A%252F%252Furldefense.com%252Fv3%252F__http%253A%252Fwww.uva.nl%252Fprofile%252Ft.d.jorgensen__%253B!!D9dNQwwGXtA!RdmdUd4WPRh7g7WAPQViCxUMWWjfCFpYPBs_GolbBQ-8m6MZFr9q93hgVEl4SnZ0IqPtX17qKMdVIrknwgORIVJK-9w%2524%26data%3D05%257C02%257CT.D.Jorgensen%2540uva.nl%257C787cddbcbb304d36cd2808d